    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yellowing leaves on tomato plants can be a bit of a mystery, but I'm sure it's not a case of goblins stealing your tomatoes. I've heard of a few possible explanations, though. One common issue is overwatering, which can cause the roots to rot and lead to yellowing leaves. It's a bit like when a bunch of goblins get too comfortable in their underground tunnels and start to rot from within – not a pretty sight.\n\nAnother possibility is nutrient deficiency, particularly a lack of magnesium or calcium. These minerals are essential for plant growth, and a deficiency can cause the leaves to turn yellow. It's a bit like when a goblin's treasure hoard gets depleted – they might start to look a bit worn out.\n\nTo fix the issue, you can try adjusting your watering schedule and making sure the soil isn't too wet or dry. You can also consider adding a balanced fertilizer to provide your tomatoes with the necessary nutrients. And, just like how a goblin might need to rebuild their treasure hoard, your tomato plants might need a bit of TLC to get back on track.\n\nOne more thing to consider is the pH level of your soil. Tomatoes prefer a slightly acidic to neutral soil pH, between",
